.TE
.SH WORDS USED HERE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]stack\f[R]: branches built on each other, e.g.
\f[CR]use\-api\f[R] built on \f[CR]add\-api\f[R], built on
\f[CR]main\f[R].
It can branch out, with several PRs built on the same one.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]parent\f[R]: the branch a branch is built on.
\f[CR]add\-api\f[R] is \f[CR]use\-api\f[R]\(aqs parent.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]root\f[R]: the branch at the bottom of a stack, usually
\f[CR]main\f[R].
git\-stack doesn\(aqt manage it, but \f[CR]sync\f[R] keeps it up to
date.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]tracked\f[R]: a branch git\-stack knows about, because you made it
with \f[CR]git stack branch\f[R], or added it with
\f[CR]git stack track\f[R] or \f[CR]git stack import\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]restack\f[R]: rebase a branch onto its parent\(aqs latest commit,
replaying only the branch\(aqs own commits.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]the current stack\f[R]: the whole stack the branch you\(aqre on is
part of, including branches beside yours, not just the ones above it,
and the other stacks in its group.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]group\f[R]: stacks that were one stack until the branch at their
bottom merged (see Groups).
.SH THE PROBLEM
PR 2 is built on PR 1\(aqs branch.
PR 1 is squash\-merged into \f[CR]main\f[R].
Now \f[CR]git rebase main\f[R] on PR 2 conflicts, because it replays PR
1\(aqs individual commits on top of a \f[CR]main\f[R] that already has
them, squashed into one commit.
git\-stack remembers where each branch\(aqs own commits start, so it
only replays those.
.SH EXAMPLE WORKFLOW
Carrying on from the Quick start, with \f[CR]add\-api\f[R] (PR 1) and
\f[CR]use\-api\f[R] (PR 2, built on PR 1).
.PP
Address review feedback on PR 1, then bring PR 2 up to date with it:
.IP
.EX
git switch add\-api
\f[I]# ...commit...\f[R]
git stack info
\f[I]# main\f[R]
\f[I]# └── add\-api *\f[R]
\f[I]#     └── use\-api (needs restack)\f[R]
git stack restack               \f[I]# rebase use\-api onto the new add\-api\f[R]
git stack push
.EE
.PP
Once PR 1 is merged:
.IP
.EX
git stack sync \-d local         \f[I]# delete add\-api, rebase use\-api onto main, switch to it, push\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]sync\f[R] only touches the current stack.
To untrack (or delete) merged branches in every stack without restacking
anything, run \f[CR]git stack clean\f[R].
.SH COMMANDS
Several commands act on the current stack: the whole stack the branch
you\(aqre on is part of, including branches beside yours.
In this stack, running \f[CR]restack\f[R] from \f[CR]pr2b\f[R] restacks
\f[CR]pr1\f[R], \f[CR]pr2a\f[R], \f[CR]pr2b\f[R] and \f[CR]pr3\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
main
└── pr1
    ├── pr2a
    │   └── pr3
    └── pr2b *
.EE
.SS Groups
When a branch on \f[CR]main\f[R] merges, the branches stacked on it each
become the bottom of a stack of their own.
So that commands still treat them as one, as before the merge,
\f[CR]sync\f[R] and \f[CR]clean\f[R] put them in a group, named after
the merged branch.
Here, \f[CR]restack\f[R] from \f[CR]pr2b\f[R] still restacks
\f[CR]pr2a\f[R], \f[CR]pr2b\f[R] and \f[CR]pr3\f[R] after \f[CR]pr1\f[R]
merges:
.IP
.EX
main
└── group pr1
    ├── pr2a
    │   └── pr3
    └── pr2b *
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
A group\(aqs stacks stay in it however many of their branches merge
later; their children join it too.
.IP \(bu 2
A group can span roots, e.g.
if you \f[CR]restack \-\-onto release\f[R] one of its stacks.
Commands on it then cover every root it\(aqs on: \f[CR]sync\f[R]
fast\-forwards each, and \f[CR]prs\f[R] gives each root\(aqs pull
requests their own stack section.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]info\f[R] only shows a group once it has two or more stacks, with
\f[CR]group <name>\f[R] above its stacks on each root they\(aqre on.
A group of one is just a stack.
.IP \(bu 2
A branch leaves its group when it\(aqs untracked, so a group ends once
its last branch is untracked.
It also leaves when it\(aqs moved onto another tracked branch (e.g.
with \f[CR]restack \-\-onto\f[R]), since it\(aqs then part of that
branch\(aqs stack, and isn\(aqt put back in the group if it\(aqs at the
bottom of a stack again later.
A branch moved from higher up a stack onto a root (e.g.
with \f[CR]restack \-\-onto main\f[R]) joins its old stack\(aqs group,
if it was in one, so the two stay together.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]git stack group\f[R] puts stacks in a group by hand, takes them
out, or renames a group.
.PP
\f[CR]restack\f[R], \f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]push\f[R] and \f[CR]prs\f[R]
act on other stacks instead of the current one when you name them, from
any branch, and \f[CR]info\f[R] shows only the stacks you name: each
argument can be a branch (its stack) or a glob of branch names, matched
like \f[CR]git branch \-\-list\f[R] does (the stacks of the branches it
matches), and \f[CR]\-g\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-group <group>\f[R], which can be
given more than once, names a group\(aqs stacks.
\f[CR]untrack\f[R] takes globs too, but untracks the branches
themselves, not their stacks, and doesn\(aqt take \f[CR]\-g\f[R].
Quote a glob, so the shell doesn\(aqt expand it:
.IP
.EX
git stack sync pr2b                     \f[I]# pr2b\(aqs stack\f[R]
git stack restack \-g search \(aqfix\-*\(aq     \f[I]# the search group, and every fix\-* branch\(aqs stack\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Like git, every command accepts \f[CR]\-C <path>\f[R] before it to run
as if started in \f[CR]<path>\f[R], e.g.
\f[CR]git stack \-C \(ti/src/app info\f[R], and
\f[CR]git \-C <path> stack ...\f[R] works too.
.SS \f[CR]git stack branch <name> [\-\-group <group>]\f[R]
Create \f[CR]<name>\f[R] on top of the current branch, switch to it, and
track it.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] Whenever you start a new branch for a
pull request, use this instead of \f[CR]git switch \-c\f[R], so
git\-stack knows what it\(aqs built on.
Run it from \f[CR]main\f[R] for a change that stands on its own, or from
another PR\(aqs branch for a change that depends on that PR before
it\(aqs merged.
.IP
.EX
git switch main
git stack branch add\-api        \f[I]# stacked on main\f[R]
git stack branch use\-api        \f[I]# stacked on add\-api\f[R]
git switch main
git stack branch search\-ui \-\-group search   \f[I]# a new stack, in group search\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Uncommitted changes come along to the new branch, as with
\f[CR]git switch \-c\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Refuses a name a remote already has a branch of (as of your last fetch),
since that\(aqs most likely someone else\(aqs branch, and pushing yours
would overwrite it.
In a shared repository, prefixing branch names with your username (e.g.
\f[CR]jane/add\-api\f[R]) avoids picking a name that\(aqs taken.
.IP \(bu 2
The new branch has no upstream until you \f[CR]git stack push\f[R] it,
even if \f[CR]branch.autoSetupMerge\f[R] would normally give it its
parent\(aqs.
.IP \(bu 2
Whatever untracked branch is at the bottom of a stack (usually
\f[CR]main\f[R]) is its root, and \f[CR]sync\f[R] and \f[CR]clean\f[R]
fast\-forward it to its upstream.
If you branch off a branch that isn\(aqt tracked, that branch becomes
the root.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-group\f[R] puts the new branch\(aqs stack in
\f[CR]<group>\f[R] (see Groups), as \f[CR]git stack group add\f[R]
would.
If the stack is in another group already, it refuses without creating
the branch.
.SS \f[CR]git stack track <parent> [<branch>...] [\-\-base <sha>]\f[R]
Track existing branches (default: the current branch) as a stack on
\f[CR]<parent>\f[R], each stacked on the one before it.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When you have branches that git\-stack
doesn\(aqt know about yet: made with plain \f[CR]git switch \-c\f[R], or
from before you installed git\-stack.
Also to record a different parent or base for a branch that\(aqs already
tracked without rebasing it, e.g.
after rebasing it by hand.
To move a branch onto another parent and rebase it in one go, use
\f[CR]git stack restack \-\-onto\f[R] instead.
.IP
.EX
git stack track main            \f[I]# stack the current branch on main\f[R]
git stack track main pr1 pr2 pr3
git stack track pr1             \f[I]# move the current branch onto pr1\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Each branch\(aqs base, the commit it was built on, is where it forked
from the branch before it (or from \f[CR]<parent>\f[R]).
Like \f[CR]git rebase\f[R], this is found from that branch\(aqs reflog,
so a parent rewritten since is handled.
Without a reflog entry, it\(aqs their merge\-base.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-base\f[R] sets the first branch\(aqs base.
Pass it if the parent was already squash\-merged, since the fork point
can\(aqt be worked out then: use the old parent tip the branch was built
on, e.g.
\f[CR]git stack track main pr2 \-\-base <old pr1 tip>\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<parent>\f[R] must be a local branch (\f[CR]main\f[R], not
\f[CR]origin/main\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
Tracking a branch that\(aqs already tracked changes its parent and base.
It doesn\(aqt rebase anything; run \f[CR]git stack restack\f[R] for
that.
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is written unless every branch can be tracked, so a bad argument
doesn\(aqt leave the stack half\-tracked.
.SS \f[CR]git stack import [<branch>...] [\-\-pr <number|url>]... [\-\-author <login>] [\-\-remote origin] [\-n | \-f]\f[R]
Track open pull requests on GitHub, drafts included, as stacks: each
pull request\(aqs branch on the branch it merges into.
They\(aqre your own, or with \f[CR]\-\-author\f[R], another GitHub
user\(aqs.
\f[CR]autocreate\f[R] is another name for it.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When you have pull requests open but
git\-stack doesn\(aqt know about their branches yet: in a fresh clone,
on another machine, or for pull requests you opened before you started
using git\-stack.
Or to build on a coworker\(aqs stack.
.IP
.EX
git stack import \-n             \f[I]# see what it would do\f[R]
git stack import
\f[I]# main\f[R]
\f[I]# └── add\-api     https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/12\f[R]
\f[I]#     └── use\-api https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/13\f[R]

git stack import \-n \-\-author alice        \f[I]# alice\(aqs stacks\f[R]
git stack import \-f \-\-author alice search \f[I]# the one with her search branch\f[R]
git stack import \-f \-\-pr 31               \f[I]# the one with pull request #31, whoever\(aqs\f[R]
git stack import \-f \-\-pr https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/31
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
It ends by showing the stacks it tracked, as \f[CR]info\f[R] does, with
the links of the pull requests whose branches it just tracked in green.
.IP \(bu 2
Branches, and pull requests by \f[CR]\-\-pr\f[R] with their number or
URL, narrow it to the stack trees they\(aqre in: everything stacked on
the bottom pull request of each, wherever in it the one you named is.
Naming someone else\(aqs pull request looks up their pull requests too;
with only \f[CR]\-\-pr\f[R], yours aren\(aqt.
.IP \(bu 2
It only looks at the open pull requests of the people it imports, from
branches in the repository: GitHub finds them with a search by author,
so other people\(aqs are never fetched, however many there are.
Pull requests from forks don\(aqt count.
It needs the GitHub adapter.
.IP \(bu 2
Someone else\(aqs work is only tracked with
\f[CR]\-f\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R]: the branches of their pull
requests, and branches without a pull request with commits by anyone but
you (by email address).
Without \f[CR]\-f\f[R], those are skipped, along with the pull requests
built on them.
Imported stacks with other contributors are marked shared;
\f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]push\f[R] and \f[CR]restack\f[R] require
\f[CR]\-\-allow\-shared\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-n\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] prints what it would do with
\f[CR]\-f\f[R], saying what needs it, without changing anything (besides
fetching).
It can\(aqt be used with \f[CR]\-f\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A pull request without a local branch gets one, created from its remote
branch, with that as its upstream.
A local branch without an upstream gets its remote branch as its
upstream.
.IP \(bu 2
Branches that are already tracked are left alone.
If one is tracked on a different parent than its pull request merges
into, it says so; run \f[CR]git stack track <parent> <branch>\f[R] if
GitHub is right.
.IP \(bu 2
If a pull request merges into a branch you don\(aqt have locally and
that has no pull request (or none it looked up), that branch is created
from its remote branch.
If merged pull requests targeted it, it is a shared root and isn\(aqt
tracked in a stack.
Otherwise it is tracked on the branch it\(aqs built on: of the
remote\(aqs branches it contains that the default branch doesn\(aqt, the
closest, or else the default branch.
If it\(aqs unclear which branch that is, the pull request is skipped,
along with the pull requests built on it; \f[CR]git switch\f[R] to that
branch and rerun to track them on it.
A branch built on an old version of its parent (one rewritten since)
looks built on what\(aqs below the parent; fix it with
\f[CR]git stack track <parent> <branch>\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Each branch\(aqs base is found as for \f[CR]git stack track\f[R], after
fast\-forwarding the branches the pull requests merge into that
aren\(aqt tracked (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]) to their upstreams, as \f[CR]sync\f[R] does.
If a parent\(aqs commits were rewritten after its child was built on
them (e.g.
the parent was rebased and force\-pushed from another machine), the
child\(aqs base can come out too old, and its next restack replays the
parent\(aqs old commits; set it with
\f[CR]git stack track <parent> <branch> \-\-base <sha>\f[R].
.SS \f[CR]git stack untrack [<branch|glob>...]\f[R]
Stop tracking branches (default: the current one), without deleting them
or changing their commits.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When you want git\-stack to stop managing
a branch but keep it around: a long\-lived branch you don\(aqt want
\f[CR]sync\f[R] to rebase, or a pull request you abandoned but might
come back to.
You don\(aqt need it for merged branches, since \f[CR]sync\f[R] and
\f[CR]clean\f[R] untrack those for you.
.IP
.EX
git stack untrack experiment
git stack untrack old\-poc old\-poc\-2
git stack untrack \(aqspike\-*\(aq             \f[I]# every tracked spike\-* branch\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Branches stacked on it stay on it, so it becomes the root of their
stack, like \f[CR]main\f[R], and \f[CR]sync\f[R] and \f[CR]clean\f[R]
fast\-forward it to its upstream.
To move them elsewhere, switch to each and run
\f[CR]git stack restack \-\-onto <parent>\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
To stop tracking a branch that was merged, use \f[CR]sync\f[R] or
\f[CR]clean\f[R], which also move its children down.
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is untracked unless every branch is tracked.
.SS \f[CR]git stack rename [<old>] <new>\f[R]
Rename a branch (default: the current one) and keep the branches stacked
on it on it.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When you want to rename a branch in a
stack.
A plain \f[CR]git branch \-m\f[R] leaves the branches stacked on it
pointing at the old name, so they\(aqd show up with a missing parent.
.IP
.EX
git stack rename api\-v2                 \f[I]# rename the current branch\f[R]
git stack rename add\-api api\-v2
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Like \f[CR]branch\f[R], refuses a new name a remote already has a branch
of.
.IP \(bu 2
Its remote branch isn\(aqt renamed, and its pull request stays with the
old name.
The renamed branch\(aqs upstream is unset, so the next
\f[CR]git stack push\f[R] pushes it under the new name, and you have to
open its pull request again.
Delete the old remote branch yourself
(\f[CR]git push origin :add\-api\f[R]) if you no longer want it.
.SS \f[CR]git stack info [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>] [\-\-json] [\-\-offline]\f[R]
Show every tracked stack (or the ones named, see Groups) as a tree, with
its root (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]) on top and the branches built on it below.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When you want to see your stacks and what
needs doing: which branches depend on which, which need a restack, and
which were merged.
It\(aqs a good first step before \f[CR]restack\f[R], \f[CR]sync\f[R] or
\f[CR]push\f[R].
.IP
.EX
git stack info
\f[I]# main\f[R]
\f[I]# └── add\-api *                   https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/12\f[R]
\f[I]#     ├── use\-api (needs restack) https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/13\f[R]
\f[I]#     └── docs (merged)           https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/14\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]*\f[R] marks the current branch.
.IP \(bu 2
To draw each stack upside down, with its root at the bottom (as
\f[CR]git log \-\-graph\f[R] and other stacking tools do), run
\f[CR]git config \-\-global stack.orientation root\-at\-bottom\f[R].
The default is \f[CR]root\-at\-top\f[R].
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
    ┌── docs (merged)
    ├── use\-api (needs restack)
┌── add\-api *
main
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR](needs restack)\f[R]: it\(aqs missing its parent\(aqs latest
commits (or, for a branch on \f[CR]main\f[R], it conflicts with
\f[CR]main\f[R]).
Run \f[CR]git stack sync\f[R] or \f[CR]git stack restack\f[R].
With color on, the branch is yellow and marked \f[CR]!\f[R] instead; the
marker makes its status visible without relying on color.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR](merged)\f[R]: it was merged into its parent (see When does
\f[CR]sync\f[R] consider a branch merged?); \f[CR]sync\f[R] or
\f[CR]clean\f[R] will stop tracking it.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR](merged, with new commits)\f[R]: it was merged, but its remote
branch got commits after the merge.
\f[CR]sync\f[R] skips it, and the branches stacked on it, until you sort
it out.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR](merged, then reverted)\f[R]: it was merged, then its parent
reverted some or all of its changes.
\f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]clean\f[R], \f[CR]restack\f[R], \f[CR]push\f[R]
and \f[CR]prs\f[R] leave it, and the branches stacked on it, alone (see
A merged branch was reverted).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR](parent <name> missing)\f[R]: its parent branch was deleted by
hand; fix it with \f[CR]git stack track <parent>\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]group <name>\f[R]: the stacks below it are in the group
\f[CR]<name>\f[R] (see Groups).
.IP \(bu 2
With the GitHub adapter, each branch\(aqs pull request (open or merged)
is linked in a column at the end of its line: blue if it\(aqs open, grey
if it\(aqs a draft, and magenta if it was merged.
.IP \(bu 2
On a terminal, \f[CR]info\f[R] and commands that show stacks use these
colors: yellow branches marked \f[CR]!\f[R] need restacking; the current
branch is bold and green unless it needs restacking; cyan notes mark
merged branches, yellow notes need attention, and red notes mark a
missing parent.
Pull request links are blue for open, grey for draft, magenta for
merged, and green when just opened or imported.
The \f[CR]*\f[R], \f[CR]!\f[R], and status text identify branch states
without relying on color.
When color is off, \f[CR](needs restack)\f[R] replaces \f[CR]!\f[R].
Like git, it follows \f[CR]color.stack\f[R] (or \f[CR]color.ui\f[R]):
set it to \f[CR]never\f[R] to turn colors off, or \f[CR]always\f[R] to
keep them when piping.
\f[CR]NO_COLOR\f[R] also turns them off.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]info\f[R] doesn\(aqt fetch, and compares each branch with its
local parent, so a merge on the remote only shows up once the local
parent has it.
\f[CR]git fetch\f[R] alone isn\(aqt enough, since it doesn\(aqt update
\f[CR]main\f[R]; run \f[CR]git stack clean\f[R] first, which fetches and
fast\-forwards \f[CR]main\f[R] (and stops tracking merged branches, but
doesn\(aqt restack anything).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] prints the same information for scripts: a list with
an object for each branch, each stack\(aqs root first, parent before
child.
\f[CR]parent\f[R] and \f[CR]status\f[R] are \f[CR]null\f[R] for a root,
and \f[CR]status\f[R] is one of \f[CR]up\-to\-date\f[R],
\f[CR]needs\-restack\f[R], \f[CR]merged\f[R],
\f[CR]merged\-with\-new\-commits\f[R], \f[CR]reverted\f[R] or
\f[CR]parent\-missing\f[R].
\f[CR]group\f[R] is the group of a branch on a stack\(aqs root, when
\f[CR]info\f[R] shows it (once the group has two or more stacks), or
\f[CR]null\f[R].
\f[CR]pr\f[R] is the branch\(aqs pull request\(aqs \f[CR]number\f[R],
\f[CR]url\f[R] and \f[CR]state\f[R] (\f[CR]open\f[R], \f[CR]draft\f[R]
or \f[CR]merged\f[R]), or \f[CR]null\f[R] if it has none (or for a root,
or without the GitHub adapter).
.RS 2
.IP
.EX
git stack info \-\-json
\f[I]# [\f[R]
\f[I]#   {\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqname\(dq: \(dqmain\(dq,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqparent\(dq: null,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqstatus\(dq: null,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqcurrent\(dq: false,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqpr\(dq: null\f[R]
\f[I]#   },\f[R]
\f[I]#   {\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqname\(dq: \(dqadd\-api\(dq,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqparent\(dq: \(dqmain\(dq,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqup\-to\-date\(dq,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqcurrent\(dq: true,\f[R]
\f[I]#     \(dqpr\(dq: {\f[R]
\f[I]#       \(dqnumber\(dq: 12,\f[R]
\f[I]#       \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttps://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/12\(dq,\f[R]
\f[I]#       \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqopen\(dq\f[R]
\f[I]#     }\f[R]
\f[I]#   },\f[R]
\f[I]#   ...\f[R]
\f[I]# ]\f[R]
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
With the GitHub adapter, \f[CR]info\f[R] asks GitHub about each
branch\(aqs pull request, which takes about a second.
\f[CR]\-\-offline\f[R] skips that: it checks for merges like the
\f[CR]git\f[R] adapter does, and shows no links.
For a shell prompt or an editor, the scripting commands below only read
git config, and are fast.
.SS \f[CR]git stack next\f[R]
Switch to the branch stacked on the current one.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] To move to the next PR in the stack, e.g.
from PR 1 to PR 2 after committing to PR 1, without remembering the
branch\(aqs name.
.IP
.EX
git switch main
git stack next                  \f[I]# to add\-api\f[R]
git stack next                  \f[I]# to use\-api\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
If several branches are stacked on the current one, it lists them and
leaves you where you are; pick one with \f[CR]git switch\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Works from a stack\(aqs root (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]), as long as only one branch is stacked on it.
.SS \f[CR]git stack prev\f[R]
Switch to the branch the current one is stacked on.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] To move to the previous PR in the stack,
e.g.
back to PR 1 to address review feedback while you\(aqre working on PR 2.
.IP
.EX
git stack prev                  \f[I]# from use\-api to add\-api\f[R]
git stack prev                  \f[I]# to main\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
From the bottom of a stack, it switches to the stack\(aqs root (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]).
.SS \f[CR]git stack ownership [show|owned|shared] [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>]\f[R]
Show the ownership of the current stack, or mark it or the named stacks
as owned or shared.
With multiple stacks, \f[CR]show\f[R] reports shared if any is shared.
A shared stack is protected against \f[CR]restack\f[R], \f[CR]sync\f[R],
and \f[CR]push\f[R] unless the command includes
\f[CR]\-\-allow\-shared\f[R].
The override is explicit on every invocation; it does not change
ownership.
Newly created branches inherit shared ownership from their parent, and
ownership follows branches when stacks split.
.PP
Import marks a stack shared when its imported commits include another
author\(aqs email, have no commits by your configured Git author, or its
pull requests include another GitHub author.
Unknown identity is treated as shared.
An explicit \f[CR]ownership owned\f[R] or \f[CR]ownership shared\f[R]
choice can change the default.
Existing stacks default to owned.
Ownership is local to this clone, not a permission or a substitute for
coordinating with contributors.
.SS \f[CR]git stack restack [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>] [\-\-onto <parent>] [\-\-squash] [\-\-latest] [\-\-allow\-shared]\f[R]
Rebase every branch in the current stack (or the ones named, see Groups)
onto its parent, bottom\-up, except that a branch on the stack\(aqs root
(e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]) only moves if it conflicts with the root\(aqs latest
commit.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] After you commit to a branch that has
other branches stacked on it, e.g.
to address review feedback on PR 1, so the branches above it include the
change.
It works locally, without fetching or pushing.
With \f[CR]\-\-onto\f[R], when a branch should be stacked somewhere
else, e.g.
PR 2 turns out not to need PR 1.
With \f[CR]\-\-squash\f[R], when a restack keeps stopping on the same
conflict, commit after commit.
With \f[CR]\-\-latest\f[R], when you want the stack on the latest
\f[CR]main\f[R] anyway, e.g.
because the repository requires pull requests to be up to date before
they\(aqre merged.
.IP
.EX
git stack restack
git stack restack \-\-onto main           \f[I]# move the current branch onto main, then restack\f[R]
git stack restack \-\-squash              \f[I]# squash each branch first, then restack\f[R]
git stack restack \-\-latest              \f[I]# also move the stack onto main\(aqs latest commit\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Only the branches\(aq own commits are replayed (from their recorded
base), so a parent that was squash\-merged doesn\(aqt cause conflicts.
See How it works.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-onto\f[R] retargets only the current branch, onto another
local branch.
The branches stacked on it come along.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-latest\f[R] moves the branches on the stack\(aqs root onto its
latest commit even if they don\(aqt conflict with it (and the rest of
the stack with them).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-squash\f[R] first squashes each branch in the current stack
into one commit, keeping all the messages and the oldest commit\(aqs
author and date, so a conflict is resolved once per branch instead of
once per commit.
It commits as \f[CR]git commit\f[R] does, running your pre\-commit and
commit\-msg hooks and signing if you\(aqve set that up.
If the commit fails (e.g.
a hook rejects it), that branch is left as it was.
.IP \(bu 2
Merged branches are skipped and their children restacked onto their
parent; \f[CR]sync\f[R] or \f[CR]clean\f[R] removes them from the stack.
.IP \(bu 2
Doesn\(aqt fetch or push.
Needs a clean worktree, and refuses to run outside a stack.
.IP \(bu 2
If a rebase stops on a conflict, see Conflicts.
.IP \(bu 2
A branch checked out in another worktree is skipped, with a warning; see
Branches checked out in other worktrees.
.SS \f[CR]git stack sync [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>] [\-\-remote origin] [\-d local|remote|both] [\-\-latest] [\-\-no\-push] [\-\-ignore\-revert <branch>] [\-\-allow\-shared] [\-\-pull\-tracked\-upstream] [\-\-rebuild\-stack]\f[R]
Bring the current stack (or the ones named, see Groups) up to date with
the remote:
.IP "1." 3
Fetch with \f[CR]\-\-prune\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Fast\-forward the stack\(aqs root (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]) to its upstream.
.IP "3." 3
Go through the stack\(aqs branches, each before the branches stacked on
it:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
stop tracking a merged one, moving its children onto its parent;
.IP \(bu 2
restack the rest, like \f[CR]restack\f[R] does: a branch on the root
only moves if it conflicts with the root\(aqs new commits, or with
\f[CR]\-\-latest\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
and pull in commits others pushed to it (see Pulling commits others
pushed).
.RE
.IP "4." 3
Push the branches that differ from the remote branch they already have,
like \f[CR]push\f[R] does.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] Your everyday command for catching up:
after a pull request in the stack was merged, when \f[CR]main\f[R]
changed in a way that conflicts with your stack, or when someone else
pushed to one of your branches.
It pushes at the end, so there\(aqs nothing to follow it with.
To catch up locally without touching the remote, use \f[CR]restack\f[R],
or \f[CR]sync \-\-no\-push\f[R].
.IP
.EX
git stack sync
git stack sync \-d local         \f[I]# also delete merged branches locally\f[R]
git stack sync \-d both          \f[I]# ...and their remote branches\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Only the current stack is synced; to untrack merged branches in every
stack, use \f[CR]clean\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
After tracking a branch with \f[CR]\-\-base\f[R], \f[CR]sync\f[R]
refuses to pull unseen commits that were already on its upstream at that
point: they could be old copies of rewritten commits.
Inspect them, then use \f[CR]\-\-pull\-tracked\-upstream\f[R] if you
want them, or \f[CR]git stack push \-\-force\f[R] if they are obsolete
and should be replaced.
The usual force\-push lease still applies.
.IP \(bu 2
It ends by showing the stacks it synced, as \f[CR]info\f[R] does.
.IP \(bu 2
Merged branches are kept unless \f[CR]\-d\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-delete\f[R]
says to delete their local branches, their remote branches, or both.
A branch is untracked by the first \f[CR]sync\f[R] or \f[CR]clean\f[R]
that sees it merged, so pass \f[CR]\-d\f[R] on that run if you want it
deleted.
Deleting a remote branch is leased to the fetched tip, so it fails
rather than deleting commits pushed since.
Only a same\-named upstream is deleted or suggested for deletion; a
different upstream is left alone.
Deleting the branch you\(aqre on switches to its first child, or to its
parent if it has none.
.IP \(bu 2
Only branches that already have a remote branch of the same name, as
\f[CR]git stack push\f[R] sets it, are pushed.
A branch that was never pushed isn\(aqt, and no pull requests are
opened: use \f[CR]git stack push\f[R] and \f[CR]git stack prs\f[R] for
those.
It never forces, so it fails if \f[CR]push\f[R] would refuse.
As with \f[CR]push\f[R], the branches are pushed in one atomic push.
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is pushed if a restack or pull stops on a conflict; rerun
\f[CR]sync\f[R] once it\(aqs resolved.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-no\-push\f[R] leaves the remote alone, and ends by listing the
branches whose remote branch is out of date.
.IP \(bu 2
A branch whose remote branch was deleted without being merged (e.g.
a closed pull request) is kept, with a warning, rather than dropping its
commits.
.IP \(bu 2
A merged branch whose remote branch got new commits after the merge is
skipped with a warning, along with the branches stacked on it, since
restacking it would replay its merged commits.
.IP \(bu 2
A merged branch its parent has since reverted is skipped with a warning
too, along with the branches stacked on it, which gives the
\f[CR]unrevert\f[R] command that brings it back (see A merged branch was
reverted).
\f[CR]\-\-ignore\-revert <branch>\f[R] (which can be given more than
once) says that wasn\(aqt really a revert: the branch counts as merged,
and is untracked as usual.
The branch has to be in the stacks being synced;
\f[CR]clean \-\-ignore\-revert\f[R] works from anywhere, and doesn\(aqt
restack or push.
.IP \(bu 2
A branch with broken metadata (e.g.
its parent was deleted by hand) is skipped with a warning, along with
everything stacked on it; the rest of the stack is still synced.
.IP \(bu 2
The root isn\(aqt fast\-forwarded if it has diverged from its upstream;
\f[CR]sync\f[R] warns instead.
.IP \(bu 2
Branches checked out in another worktree are left alone, with a warning;
see Branches checked out in other worktrees.
\f[CR]sync\f[R] pushes the rest, then fails, listing the branches it
couldn\(aqt push for that reason (unless it was run with
\f[CR]\-\-no\-push\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
Needs a clean worktree, and refuses to run outside a stack.
.SS \f[CR]git stack clean [\-\-remote origin] [\-d local|remote|both] [\-\-ignore\-revert <branch>]\f[R]
Fetch with \f[CR]\-\-prune\f[R], fast\-forward every stack\(aqs root,
and stop tracking merged branches in every stack, moving their children
onto their parent.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] For housekeeping across every stack at
once, e.g.
after pull requests in several stacks were merged, without having to
resolve conflicts in stacks you\(aqre not working on right now.
For the stack you\(aqre working on, use \f[CR]sync\f[R].
.IP
.EX
git stack clean
git stack clean \-d local
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is restacked, so there are no conflicts.
The children are rebased the next time you \f[CR]sync\f[R] or
\f[CR]restack\f[R] their stack.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-d\f[R] and \f[CR]\-\-ignore\-revert\f[R] work as for
\f[CR]sync\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
It ends by showing every stack, as \f[CR]info\f[R] does.
.IP \(bu 2
Runs from any branch, even one that isn\(aqt in a stack.
Uncommitted changes are fine, unless you\(aqre on a stack\(aqs root,
which \f[CR]clean\f[R] fast\-forwards, or, with \f[CR]\-d local\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-d both\f[R], on a tracked branch, which it could delete.
.SS \f[CR]git stack push [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>] [\-\-remote origin] [\-\-force] [\-\-prs] [\-\-allow\-shared] [\-\-rebuild\-stack]\f[R]
Force\-push (with lease) every branch in the current stack (or the ones
named, see Groups), and set each one\(aqs upstream.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] Whenever the remote should match your
local branches: after committing, after a \f[CR]restack\f[R], and for a
branch \f[CR]sync\f[R] doesn\(aqt push because it was never pushed.
Use it instead of \f[CR]git push\f[R], since restacking rewrites the
branches\(aq history.
It also keeps pull requests pointed at the right base, and records what
it pushed, so \f[CR]sync\f[R] won\(aqt pull the old copies of your
rebased commits back in.
.IP
.EX
git stack push
git stack push use\-api          \f[I]# push use\-api\(aqs stack from anywhere\f[R]
git stack push \(aqsearch\-*\(aq       \f[I]# the stacks of every search\-* branch\f[R]
git stack push \-\-force          \f[I]# drop commits on the remote you didn\(aqt pull\f[R]
git stack push \-\-prs            \f[I]# then open pull requests, as \(gagit stack prs\(ga does\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Each branch is pushed to a remote branch of the same name.
.IP \(bu 2
It ends by showing the stacks it pushed, as \f[CR]info\f[R] does.
.IP \(bu 2
Refuses, even with \f[CR]\-\-force\f[R], if that remote branch exists
but isn\(aqt the branch\(aqs upstream, since it\(aqs probably someone
else\(aqs branch that happens to have the same name.
Rename yours with \f[CR]git stack rename\f[R], or, if the remote branch
is yours (say you pushed it with a plain \f[CR]git push\f[R]), set it as
the upstream with \f[CR]git branch \-\-set\-upstream\-to\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
Merged branches are skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
Refuses if a remote branch has commits the local one doesn\(aqt, so you
don\(aqt overwrite a collaborator\(aqs work: run
\f[CR]git stack sync\f[R] to pull them in first, or pass
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] to drop them.
Either way, each lease is pinned to the remote\-tracking ref that was
checked, so if someone pushes after your last fetch, the push fails
instead of overwriting their work.
.IP \(bu 2
A remote branch whose commits were rebased there, e.g.
by GitHub\(aqs \(dqRebase stack\(dq button, isn\(aqt pushed over: the
local branch is moved to it instead, as \f[CR]sync\f[R] does (see
Rebased on the remote).
\f[CR]\-\-force\f[R] pushes over it.
.IP \(bu 2
The branches are pushed in one atomic push: if any of them can\(aqt be
pushed (e.g.
because its remote branch changed since your last fetch), none are.
.IP \(bu 2
Refuses to push a branch whose remote branch was deleted, since
that\(aqs usually a merged pull request \f[CR]sync\f[R] hasn\(aqt seen
yet.
If it wasn\(aqt, run \f[CR]git branch \-\-unset\-upstream <branch>\f[R]
to push it again.
.IP \(bu 2
With the GitHub adapter, points each open pull request at its stack
parent (e.g.
at \f[CR]main\f[R] once the parent PR was merged), and lists the
branches that have none, which \f[CR]git stack prs\f[R] opens.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-\-prs\f[R] (or \f[CR]\-\-pr\f[R]) then does what
\f[CR]git stack prs\f[R] does for the stacks it pushed, so it\(aqs
\f[CR]git stack push\f[R] and \f[CR]git stack prs\f[R] in one.
Without the GitHub adapter, it still pushes and shows the stacks, but
then fails, saying it didn\(aqt open or update any pull requests.
.SS \f[CR]git stack group add <name> [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>] [\-\-move]\f[R]
Put stacks (default: the current one) in group \f[CR]<name>\f[R], so
\f[CR]restack\f[R], \f[CR]sync\f[R], \f[CR]push\f[R] and \f[CR]prs\f[R]
on one of them cover them all, and so you can name them all at once (see
Groups).
\f[CR]git stack group remove\f[R] takes stacks out of their groups
(\f[CR]git stack ungroup\f[R] for short), and
\f[CR]git stack group rename [<old>] <new>\f[R] renames a group
(default: the current stack\(aqs).
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When you work on several stacks together,
e.g.
a feature split across stacks that don\(aqt depend on each other, or to
rename a group \f[CR]sync\f[R] made after a merge.
.IP
.EX
git stack group add search \(aqsearch\-*\(aq   \f[I]# the stacks of every search\-* branch\f[R]
git stack sync \-g search                \f[I]# from anywhere\f[R]
git stack group rename search find      \f[I]# or, from one of its stacks, \(gagroup rename find\(ga\f[R]
git stack ungroup search\-ui             \f[I]# take search\-ui\(aqs stack back out\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
The arguments name stacks as for \f[CR]sync\f[R] (branches, globs, and
groups with \f[CR]\-g\f[R]), and each stack goes in by its bottom
branch, the one on its root.
.IP \(bu 2
A stack can only be in one group.
If any of them is in another group already, \f[CR]add\f[R] refuses,
listing them, since a glob can easily match a stack you didn\(aqt mean
to take out of its group.
\f[CR]\-\-move\f[R] moves them out of it.
.IP \(bu 2
A group name can\(aqt be empty or have spaces in it.
It can be the same as a branch\(aqs name, since groups are only ever
named with \f[CR]\-g\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]rename\f[R] refuses a name another group has, so two groups
aren\(aqt merged by mistake.
To merge them, run
\f[CR]git stack group add \-\-move <name> \-g <other group>\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]info\f[R] shows a group once it has two or more stacks.
.SS \f[CR]git stack prs [<branch|glob>...] [\-g <group>] [\-\-remote origin] [\-n] [\-\-rebuild\-stack]\f[R]
Open a draft pull request on GitHub for each branch in the current stack
(or the ones named, see Groups) that has none, into its stack parent,
bottom\-up.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] After pushing a new stack, or new
branches in one, to open their pull requests with the right bases.
\f[CR]git stack push \-\-prs\f[R] pushes and opens them in one go.
.IP
.EX
git stack push
git stack prs \-n                \f[I]# see what it would open\f[R]
git stack prs
\f[I]# main\f[R]
\f[I]# └── add\-api *       https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/12\f[R]
\f[I]#     └── use\-api     https://github.com/acme/widgets/pull/13\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
It ends by showing the stacks, as \f[CR]info\f[R] does, with the links
of the pull requests it just opened in green.
.IP \(bu 2
Each pull request\(aqs title is its branch\(aqs oldest commit\(aqs
subject, and its body the repository\(aqs pull request template,
\f[CR]pull_request_template.md\f[R] (in any case) in
\f[CR].github/\f[R], the repository\(aqs root or \f[CR]docs/\f[R], as on
the remote\(aqs default branch.
Templates in a \f[CR]PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE/\f[R] directory aren\(aqt
used, as GitHub only uses them when asked for by name.
.IP \(bu 2
Branches that already have a pull request (open or merged), merged
branches, and branches with no commits of their own are skipped.
An open pull request that isn\(aqt into its branch\(aqs stack parent
(say it was opened from the link GitHub prints on push, which targets
\f[CR]main\f[R]) is pointed at it, as \f[CR]push\f[R] does.
.IP \(bu 2
Then it adds the stack to the end of each open pull request\(aqs
description, as a tree from the stack\(aqs root, with that pull request
marked, and keeps it up to date each time it runs, showing each open one
on what it\(aqs stacked on now.
Merged pull requests are left out, except the one each pull request on
the stack\(aqs root was last stacked on (whose branch its base was
changed from after it merged), marked as merged.
That one\(aqs description still has the stack as it was when it merged,
with the one it was stacked on, so the links lead back through the
earlier pull requests.
A stack with only one pull request, counting that one, gets none, and
one it had is removed.
Text you add outside the stack is kept, but edits to the stack itself
are overwritten.
.IP \(bu 2
If the repository has GitHub\(aqs \c
.UR https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/reference/stacked-pull-requests
stacked pull requests
.UE \c
, and a stack\(aqs open pull requests are a chain, each on the one
before, it first links them into a stack on GitHub, or adds the new ones
to the top of the one they\(aqre in.
Once they\(aqre all in one, GitHub shows the stack on each pull request,
so they get no stack in their descriptions, and the ones they had are
removed.
A stack that branches out, or a group\(aqs stacks, can\(aqt be one stack
on GitHub, so they keep theirs.
Merged pull requests can\(aqt be taken out of a stack on GitHub, so if
some are in one that doesn\(aqt match (e.g.
in another order, or ending with a merged pull request that a new one
isn\(aqt based on), it warns and leaves them, using description sections
instead.
GitHub doesn\(aqt change the base of a pull request in a stack either.
When a stack is reshaped (e.g.
a branch is put in its middle), \f[CR]push\f[R], \f[CR]sync\f[R], and
\f[CR]prs\f[R] refuse to unstack it to change those bases unless given
\f[CR]\-\-rebuild\-stack\f[R].
That option can unstack other open pull requests in the same GitHub
stack.
Run \f[CR]prs\f[R] to link them again, or use
\f[CR]push \-\-prs \-\-rebuild\-stack\f[R] to do both together.
\f[CR]sync\f[R] may already have changed local branches before it
reaches this check.
To stop it linking them, run
\f[CR]git config stack.githubStacks false\f[R].
git\-stack links them through GitHub\(aqs API, with \f[CR]gh\f[R], so it
doesn\(aqt need GitHub\(aqs own extension for stacks, \c
.UR https://github.com/github/gh-stack
gh\-stack
.UE \c
\ (\f[CR]gh extension install github/gh\-stack\f[R]).
.RS 2
.PP
Use \f[CR]git stack prs \-\-rebuild\-stack\f[R] to unstack the selected
pull requests on GitHub and create a new stack when the existing stack
doesn\(aqt match.
Merged pull requests remain in the old stack.
It refuses if unstacking would also remove an unmerged pull request
outside the selected stack.
If creating the new stack fails, the pull requests remain unstacked;
rerun \f[CR]prs\f[R] to retry.
Add \f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] to see the planned unstack and creation
without changing them.
.IP
.EX
<!\-\- git\-stack \-\->
<!\-\- Kept up to date by \(gagit stack prs\(ga, which overwrites any edits to this section. \-\->
\-\-\-
**Stack**
\- \(gamain\(ga
  \- #12 (merged)
    \- #13 👈
      \- #14
<!\-\- /git\-stack \-\->
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Refuses unless every branch in the stack has been pushed with
\f[CR]git stack push\f[R] and matches its remote branch, so each pull
request shows what you have locally, as of your last fetch.
Also refuses if the stack\(aqs root (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]) isn\(aqt on the remote, since the pull requests at the
bottom of the stack merge into it.
.IP \(bu 2
If opening one fails, it says which it had opened already; rerun it once
the problem is fixed to open the rest.
.IP \(bu 2
Needs the GitHub adapter.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]git stack pr\f[R] is an alias.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-n\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-dry\-run\f[R] shows the stacks with the pull
requests it would open, with their titles, in place of their links,
without opening them.
.SS \f[CR]git stack unrevert <name> <revert>... [\-\-from <branch>] [\-\-stack <branch>...] [\-\-remote origin]\f[R]
Create \f[CR]<name>\f[R] on \f[CR]\-\-from\f[R], with commits that
revert the given reverts, to bring back the changes of a merged branch
they reverted, and move the branches given with \f[CR]\-\-stack\f[R]
onto it.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] When a pull request was merged and then
reverted, and you want to bring its changes back, e.g.
to fix whatever got it reverted and merge it again, with the pull
requests built on it on top.
While the reverted branch is still tracked, \f[CR]sync\f[R],
\f[CR]clean\f[R], \f[CR]restack\f[R], \f[CR]push\f[R] and \f[CR]prs\f[R]
warn about it with the command to run; see A merged branch was reverted
for when it isn\(aqt.
.IP
.EX
git stack unrevert add\-api\-unrevert 1a2b3c4d5e6f \-\-stack use\-api
\f[I]# created add\-api\-unrevert on main, reverting 1 revert\f[R]
\f[I]# moved use\-api onto it\f[R]
\f[I]# note: run \(gagit stack push \-\-prs\(ga to open a pull request for add\-api\-unrevert\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<name>\f[R] must be a new branch, and like \f[CR]branch\f[R], it
refuses a name a remote already has a branch of.
It\(aqs tracked on \f[CR]\-\-from\f[R], which defaults to the root of
the first \f[CR]\-\-stack\f[R] branch\(aqs stack (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]), or without \f[CR]\-\-stack\f[R], to the remote\(aqs
default branch.
.IP \(bu 2
The reverts are reverted newest first, whatever order you give them in,
so one that undid what a later one redid doesn\(aqt conflict.
Each must be in \f[CR]\-\-from\f[R]\(aqs history.
The new commits get git\(aqs usual messages for reverting a revert;
amend them as you like.
.IP \(bu 2
Each \f[CR]\-\-stack\f[R] branch moves onto \f[CR]<name>\f[R] with the
branches stacked on it, keeping its own commits, and is restacked onto
it.
That changes its pull request\(aqs base the next time you push.
A merged, reverted branch they leave with nothing else stacked on it is
untracked, but not deleted.
.IP \(bu 2
If a revert stops on a conflict, resolve it, run
\f[CR]git revert \-\-continue\f[R], then \f[CR]git stack restack\f[R] to
move the \f[CR]\-\-stack\f[R] branches onto \f[CR]<name>\f[R].
They were moved before the reverts, so \f[CR]git revert \-\-abort\f[R]
instead leaves them on \f[CR]<name>\f[R] with none of the reverted
changes.
.IP \(bu 2
Works on the local \f[CR]\-\-from\f[R], without fetching, so run
\f[CR]git stack sync\f[R] first if the revert only happened on the
remote.
Needs a clean worktree.
.SH SCRIPTING
The commands below print one thing per line, for use in scripts,
aliases, shell prompts and editors.
They only read git config, so they\(aqre fast, and they\(aqre safe to
run while another git\-stack command is running.
They fail with exit status 1 for a branch that isn\(aqt in a stack.
.PP
They don\(aqt change any branch.
The only thing they write, like every command, is the result of a
restack or pull you finished by hand after a conflict.
.PP
Every command can also print JSON; see JSON output.
.SS \f[CR]git stack parent [<branch>]\f[R]
Print the branch that \f[CR]<branch>\f[R] (default: the current branch)
is stacked on.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] To compare a branch with what it\(aqs
built on, the way its pull request does:
.IP
.EX
git diff $(git stack parent)...         \f[I]# what this branch\(aqs pull request changes\f[R]
git log \-\-oneline $(git stack parent)..
.EE
.SS \f[CR]git stack base [<branch>]\f[R]
Print the commit \f[CR]<branch>\f[R] (default: the current branch) was
last rebased onto: where its own commits start.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] To act on just a branch\(aqs own commits.
Unlike \f[CR]git stack parent\f[R], this still works after the parent
was squash\-merged or rewritten, since it doesn\(aqt depend on the
parent\(aqs current history:
.IP
.EX
git log \-\-oneline $(git stack base)..   \f[I]# this branch\(aqs own commits\f[R]
git rebase \-i $(git stack base)         \f[I]# reword or reorder them\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
It\(aqs the commit \f[CR]restack\f[R] replays the branch\(aqs commits
from, so it\(aqs only out of date while a restack is stopped on a
conflict.
.SS \f[CR]git stack show\-stack [\-q] [<branch>]\f[R]
Print the branches in \f[CR]<branch>\f[R]\(aqs (default: the current
branch\(aqs) stack, bottom first, one per line.
The stack\(aqs root (e.g.
\f[CR]main\f[R]) isn\(aqt included.
.PP
\f[B]When would I use it?\f[R] To run something on every branch in a
stack, or, with \f[CR]\-q\f[R], to check whether a branch is in a stack
at all:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]for\f[R] branch \f[B]in\f[R] $(git stack show\-stack)\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]do\f[R]
    git switch \-\-quiet \(dq$branch\(dq \f[B]&&\f[R] make test \f[B]||\f[R] \f[B]break\f[R]
\f[B]done\f[R]

\f[B]if\f[R] git stack show\-stack \-q\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    echo \(dqin a stack\(dq
\f[B]fi\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\-q\f[R]/\f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R] prints nothing, not even an error:
it only exits with status 0 if the branch is in a stack, and 1 if it
isn\(aqt.
.IP \(bu 2
As with the other commands, the stack includes branches beside the
current one, not just those above and below it.
.SS JSON output
Every command takes \f[CR]\-\-json\f[R], for scripts and tools that run
them.
In place of its usual output, it prints a JSON object per line (\c
.UR https://jsonlines.org
JSON Lines
.UE \c
) as it goes: one for each thing that happens, then one with the result.
git\(aqs own output, e.g.
from fetching or rebasing, goes to stderr.
(\f[CR]info \-\-json\f[R] is older, and prints a single list; see
\f[CR]info\f[R].)
.IP
.EX
git stack clean \-d local \-\-json
\f[I]# {\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqfetching\(dq, \(dqmessage\(dq: \(dqfetching origin, and pruning its deleted branches\(dq, \(dqwarning\(dq: false, \(dqremote\(dq: \(dqorigin\(dq}\f[R]
\f[I]# {\(dqtype\(dq: \(dquntracked\(dq, \(dqmessage\(dq: \(dqpr1 was merged into main; deleted it (was 0123456789ab) and moved pr2 onto main\(dq, \(dqwarning\(dq: false, \(dqbranch\(dq: \(dqpr1\(dq, \(dqparent\(dq: \(dqmain\(dq, \(dqchildren\(dq: [\(dqpr2\(dq], \(dqdeleted_tip\(dq: \(dq0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\(dq}\f[R]
\f[I]# {\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqresult\(dq, \(dqstacks\(dq: [...], \(dqevents\(dq: [...]}\f[R]

git stack parent \-\-json
\f[I]# {\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqresult\(dq, \(dqparent\(dq: \(dqmain\(dq, \(dqevents\(dq: []}\f[R]
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Each thing that happens has a \f[CR]type\f[R], the \f[CR]message\f[R]
the command would print, whether it\(aqs a \f[CR]warning\f[R], and
fields that depend on its type.
For example, \f[CR]untracked\f[R] (a merged branch was untracked) has
the merged \f[CR]branch\f[R], the \f[CR]parent\f[R] its
\f[CR]children\f[R] were moved onto, and \f[CR]deleted_tip\f[R], the
commit the branch pointed at if it was deleted, or \f[CR]null\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The last line\(aqs \f[CR]type\f[R] is \f[CR]result\f[R], with what the
command did (see below), and \f[CR]events\f[R]: everything that happened
again, so a script that only reads the last line has all of it.
If the command fails, the last line is
\f[CR]{\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqerror\(dq, \(dqmessage\(dq: ..., \(dqevents\(dq: [...]}\f[R]
instead, and it exits with status 1.
The exception is \f[CR]push \-\-prs\f[R] that pushed, but couldn\(aqt
open pull requests: its last line is still its result, with an
\f[CR]error\f[R], and it exits with status 1.
Arguments it doesn\(aqt understand print no JSON: just usage, on stderr,
with exit status 2.
.IP \(bu 2
Types and field names don\(aqt change, though new ones may be added.
.PP
